(Bug #74's control. The type lock could not see through a `'s <property>`
 read, so it let a mismatched destination through and the program faulted on
 the first read. Teaching it the property tables must not narrow what a
 CORRECT property read is allowed to say: every line below is a property read
 landing in the type LANGUAGE.md's own tables give it, and every one of them
 compiled and printed exactly this before the fix too.)

a list called counts is [10, 20, 30].
a map called ages is {"ada": 36, "bea": 41}.

(List Properties - `length` and `size` are one property under two names, both
 Number; `empty` is Boolean.)
a number called tally is counts's length.
a number called 'the same tally' is counts's size.
a boolean called drained is counts's empty.
print tally.
print 'the same tally'.
print drained.

(Map Properties - a map answers `length` the way a list does, and `keys` and
 `values` build fresh lists.)
a number called 'people named' is ages's length.
a list called who is ages's keys.
a list called 'how old' is ages's values.
print 'people named'.
print who.
print 'how old'.

(Buffer Properties - `size`, `capacity`, `empty` and `full`.)
a buffer called ledger is 16 bytes in size.
append "abc" to ledger.
a number called 'bytes held' is ledger's size.
a number called room is ledger's capacity.
a boolean called blank is ledger's empty.
a boolean called brimming is ledger's full.
print 'bytes held'.
print room.
print blank.
print brimming.

(Number Properties - the five questions are Boolean, `sign` is Number.)
a number called gauge is 0 subtract 42.
a boolean called halves is gauge's even.
a boolean called 'leaves one' is gauge's odd.
a boolean called 'above zero' is gauge's positive.
a boolean called 'below zero' is gauge's negative.
a boolean called 'at zero' is gauge's zero.
a number called direction is gauge's sign.
print halves.
print 'leaves one'.
print 'above zero'.
print 'below zero'.
print 'at zero'.
print direction.

(The universal `type` property reports text, and lands in a text.)
a text called described is gauge's type.
print described.

(`first` and `last` are typed by the LIST, not by the property - bug #54's
 machinery, untouched here. A list of numbers hands back a number.)
a number called leading is counts's first.
a number called trailing is counts's last.
print leading.
print trailing.

(`absolute` is typed by its base too - a float's absolute is a float - so it
 stays unproven and unchecked, exactly as before.)
a number called distance is gauge's absolute.
print distance.

(Time Properties are Number. The clock moves, so these are read into the
 numbers they are and then compared, not printed.)
Get current time into now.
a number called 'this year' is now's year.
a number called 'this hour' is now's hour.
If 'this year' is greater than 2023 then, print "the year is a number".
If 'this hour' is greater than -1 then, print "the hour is a number".

(Timer Properties - `start time` and `end time` are Number, `running` is
 Boolean, and `duration` is a Duration that reads through a unit cast.)
a timer called clock.
Start clock.
Stop clock.
a number called 'began at' is clock's start time.
a boolean called 'still going' is clock's running.
a number called 'seconds taken' is clock's duration in seconds.
If 'began at' is greater than 0 then, print "the start time is a number".
print 'still going'.
If 'seconds taken' is greater than -1 then, print "the duration is a number".

(File Properties - `size`, `descriptor`, `permissions` and the two mode
 questions.)
open a file for writing called sink at "./test_425_three_bytes.txt".
Write "abc" to sink.
Close sink.
open a file for reading called source at "./test_425_three_bytes.txt".
a number called 'file bytes' is source's size.
a number called handle is source's descriptor.
a number called mode is source's permissions.
a boolean called 'can read' is source's readable.
a boolean called 'can write' is source's writable.
print 'file bytes'.
If handle is greater than 2 then, print "the descriptor is a number".
If mode is greater than 0 then, print "the permissions are a number".
print 'can read'.
print 'can write'.
Close source.
Delete the file "./test_425_three_bytes.txt".

(And the cast is the other documented way to land a property read somewhere
 else - LANGUAGE.md's Basic Conversions.)
a text called spelled is counts's length as text.
print spelled.
